    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my ficus ginseng bonsai dropping leaves?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ficus ginseng drops leaves when its conditions change or stress it. University of Missouri Extension names changes in its surroundings, cold and uneven watering, and UF/IFAS Extension adds soil that stays too wet and low light. Some leaf drop after delivery or a move is normal; it is a problem only if it continues."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my ficus ginseng leaves turning yellow while the soil stays wet?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yellow leaves on a ficus ginseng whose soil stays wet usually mean too much water. NC State Extension names overwatering as a cause of root rot in Ficus microcarpa, and University of Missouri Extension says too much water suffocates the roots."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are the new leaves on my ficus ginseng curled, folded and spotted purple?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "New leaves that curl and fold along the middle vein, with dark brown, reddish or purplish scars, are galls made by Cuban laurel thrips. University of California IPM says this thrips mainly attacks Ficus microcarpa. Small black insects and pale larvae live inside the folded leaves. The damage is mostly cosmetic, but heavy feeding can slow growth and make leaves fall early."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What are the white specks and fine webs on my ficus ginseng?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Tiny white flecks on ficus leaves, sometimes with fine webbing, are a sign of spider mites. NC State Extension lists spider mites as a pest of Ficus microcarpa, and Colorado State University Extension says their feeding wounds appear as white flecks."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my ficus ginseng leaves sticky, with brown bumps or white fluff?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Sticky leaves with small brown bumps on the stems and leaf veins are a sign of scale insects; white, cottony clusters are mealybugs. NC State Extension lists both as pests of Ficus microcarpa. Both suck sap and leave a sticky honeydew on the leaves below."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my ficus ginseng leaves dry and shrivelled?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Dry, shrivelled leaves on a ficus ginseng come from too much sunlight or very low humidity, according to UF/IFAS Extension. In the UAE this can happen when a bonsai sits in afternoon sun behind glass or outdoors in summer without shade."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is a ficus ginseng the same as Ficus retusa?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Ginseng ficus is often sold labelled Ficus retusa, but NC State Extension says that is a common mislabel: the ginseng ficus is a cultivar of Ficus microcarpa, and the name Ficus retusa belongs to another plant. So a ginseng bonsai labelled 'Ficus retusa' is still a Ficus microcarpa and follows the advice in this guide."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is a ficus ginseng a good bonsai for beginners?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. NC State Extension says the ginseng ficus grows somewhat faster and is easier to care for than other bonsai species, which makes it a good first bonsai. It needs bright light, steady watering and a spot out of draughts more than special bonsai skills."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does a ficus ginseng clean the air?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ficus ginseng does not noticeably clean the air in a normal room. A 2019 review of chamber studies (Cummings and Waring) found that a building would need 10 to 1,000 potted plants per square metre of floor to match the air exchange it already gets from ventilation."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is it called a ginseng ficus?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ginseng ficus is named after its thick, bulbous roots, which look like ginseng roots. NC State Extension says these exposed roots are the reason the cultivar is used so widely for bonsai. It is a fig, not a ginseng plant."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why does my ficus ginseng grow thin roots from its branches?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Thin roots that hang from the branches are aerial roots, which are normal for Ficus microcarpa. NC State Extension says high humidity favours them. You can leave them or remove them: University of Missouri Extension says unwanted aerial roots can be removed without harming the plant."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How big does a ficus ginseng bonsai get?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ficus ginseng bonsai stays small because it grows in a small pot and is pruned regularly. The same species, Ficus microcarpa, grows into a large tree in warm climates: NC State Extension says it can reach about 12 m (40 ft) outdoors in Mediterranean climates."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is the sap of a ficus ginseng harmful to people?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "The white, milky sap (latex) of a ficus can irritate the skin. NC State Extension says ficus latex can cause minor skin irritation, especially in sunlight. Wear gloves when you prune if your skin is sensitive, wash your hands afterwards, and keep the plant away from young children who might chew it."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is a ficus ginseng bonsai a good gift?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ficus ginseng bonsai suits people who have a bright window and enjoy small care jobs such as pruning. Tell the person that some leaf drop in the first weeks is normal, and that the plant is toxic to cats and dogs."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "My plant is dropping leaves after arrival. Should I worry?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Some leaf drop is normal as a plant adjusts to new light and temperature, especially Fiddle Leaf Fig. Keep it in one spot, avoid frequent moves, and keep watering steady."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "My plant arrived with a few drooping or yellow leaves. Is that normal?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes, that's normal. A few drooping, bent or yellow leaves are a common reaction to the journey and a new spot, and plants usually recover within a few days. Keep it in bright indirect light and check the soil before watering. If the plant or pot is actually damaged, send photos within 24 hours."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Will my plant look exactly like the website photo?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "You'll receive the variety and size you ordered, but every plant grows individually, so leaf count, shape and shade can differ from the photo, which is representative. For peace of mind, ask for a pre-dispatch photo at checkout or on WhatsApp."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Do the decorative pots have drainage holes?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Decorative pot orders include a drainage layer at the base, with premium soil mix and perlite. For specific details on a particular pot model, send us the product link and our team will confirm."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Which pot option should I choose, nursery or decorative?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Choose the nursery pot for the lowest price or if you already have a planter. Choose a decorative pot for a ready-to-display plant that arrives fully potted with soil mix, perlite and a drainage layer. The plant itself is the same."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is the plant healthy on arrival? Can I check first?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Check your plant with the delivery agent: if it's damaged or not what you ordered, you can refuse it on the spot. If you find damage after unpacking, send photos and your order number on WhatsApp (+971 4 294 1422) or email sales@plantsworld.ae within 24 hours and we'll replace it free. You can also ask for a pre-dispatch photo."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Do I need to repot my plant when it arrives?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Not immediately. Let it settle for a few weeks first, and note that a repotted plant can't be returned or replaced, so check it's healthy before you repot. Plants in decorative pots arrive fully potted. Repot only when roots fill the pot or growth stops."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can I send a plant as a gift to someone?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. Enter the recipient's address and phone number at checkout. Decorative pot options arrive fully potted, ready to display. Gift orders can't be returned or cancelled for change of mind, but if a gift arrives damaged we replace it free."
     }
    }
   ]
